// Waterbirth Island Dungeon - entrance, ladder and the pressure-door puzzle.
// Design/coordinates: claude/horror-from-the-deep-quest.md, "The basement, found" (full 660-region
// map sweep) and "What's actually left" below it. See area_waterbirth.constant for every coordinate
// used here, all re-derived from content/maps/m39_58.jm2 / m38_158.jm2 / m39_158.jm2.
//
// Real wiki mechanic (https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Waterbirth_Island_Dungeon) is a two-player
// pressure-pad puzzle: "one player must stand on a button while their companion stands on the
// other" to open one of three doors gating the main cavern. That's exactly what the cache has
// placed here - dagannoth_pressure_door/_door2/_door3 (real op1=Open) each paired with two
// dagannoth_pressure_pad_1/_2 tiles (forcedecor=yes, no op - pure presence-detection tiles, not
// clickable) - so this is implemented as a genuine two-player check via map_playercount on both
// pad coordinates, not guessed at.
//
// CORRECTION (superseded, kept for history): I originally assumed dagannoth_weak_door_south/west/
// north (npc ids 2440/2443/2446, clustered right next to the ladder in m39_158) were unusable
// because every state has vislevel=hide + minimap=no. Corey confirmed in-game they DO render and
// ARE attackable, so that assumption was wrong - vislevel=hide evidently doesn't mean "never
// rendered" the way I read it. These are real, separate content from the pressure-door puzzle above
// (a different corridor cluster, m39_158 not m38_158) - see the "Door-support puzzle" section below
// for how they're wired now.
//
// Deliberately NOT modelled this pass (matching the wiki's fuller description, flagged rather than
// guessed): the "can't open the pad facing your own approach" nuance, and the pet-rock/scrambled-egg
// solo substitute for a second pad-presence - solo players need someone else online to open a door
// for now.
//
// UPDATE (second pass): the main cavern and the Kings' lair are now wired up too. Found a real
// 34-piece numbered ladder network (dagexp_ladder1-34 + dagexp_entrance_ladder/_exit_ladder/
// _bossroomladder_up/_down) spanning m28_68/m29_68/m30_68's four planes (0-3), plus a genuine
// cross-region pair (dagexp_bossroomladder_down/_up) connecting straight into the Kings' lair at
// m45_69. See area_waterbirth.constant for the full derivation and pairing evidence. The one gap
// still bridged by inference rather than a placed connector: crossing from the pressure-door room
// (m38_158/m39_158) into the cavern's dagexp_entrance_ladder - no loc was found linking these two
// void-pocket clusters directly, so dagannoth_ladder_base (which has no narrative reason to lead
// back to the surface) was repurposed as that bridge instead, and dagannoth_rocky_steps (real desc
// "Leads to the surface") takes over the surface-return job that dagannoth_ladder_base held in the
// first pass of this file.

// --- Entrance (Rellekka coast, m39_58) ---
// Corey-supplied coords (2026-09-03): the two entrances land in different spots after all - rock
// goes to the main/"normal" cavern (m38_158), ice lands right by the iron ladder (m39_158), tying
// the ice entrance and the ladder together as requested.
[oploc1,dagannoth_caveentrance_rock]
mes("You duck into the dark, cold cave.");
p_telejump(^waterbirth_entrance_rock_dest);

[oploc1,dagannoth_caveentrance_ice]
mes("You duck into the dark, cold cave.");
p_telejump(^waterbirth_entrance_ice_dest);


// --- Ladder (dagannoth_ladder_base, m39_158) and steps (dagannoth_rocky_steps, m38_158) ---
// dagannoth_ladder_base (real name="Iron ladder", desc="I could climb this if I wanted" - generic,
// no "surface" framing) now leads deeper, into the cavern's dagexp_entrance_ladder spot - there's no
// placed loc directly bridging these two void-pocket clusters, so this is an inferred jump, not
// confirmed placement data (see header comment/project doc).
// The cache actually gives this loc three ops (op1=Climb, op2=Climb-up, op3=Climb-down) - only op1
// was wired originally, so right-clicking Climb-down did nothing ("nothing interesting happens").
// All three now resolve: op1/op3 both go deeper (op1 kept as the ambiguous default, matching op3);
// op2 goes back to the surface (reusing the rock/steps surface_return - Corey hasn't given a
// distinct ice-side surface point, so this is a reasonable default until/unless he wants Climb-up
// to specifically pop back out the ice cave instead).
//
// SECOND BUG FOUND (2026-09-03): dagannoth_ladder_base is only the UPPER half of this ladder
// (plane 1, local(50,31) per the .jm2 sweep). The tile players actually stand on and click - plane
// 0, same local(50,31) - is a SEPARATE loc, dagannoth_ladder_base_ext2 (same name/desc/three ops,
// but a different debugname to the compiler). Since only dagannoth_ladder_base had handlers, anyone
// clicking the ladder from ground level got "nothing interesting happens" regardless of which op -
// this is almost certainly what Corey was hitting both times he reported the ladder as broken.
// Wired identically to the base piece below.
//
// THIRD PASS (2026-09-03): op1 now pops the real "Climb Up./Climb Down." choice dialogue via the
// shared @ladder_options label (content/scripts/ladders+stairs/scripts/ladders.rs2 - the same
// pattern used by grandtree_laddermiddle/laddermiddle/qip_cook_ladder_middle elsewhere in this
// codebase), with op2/op3 kept as direct shortcuts for the same two destinations - matching the
// cache's real op1=Climb/op2=Climb-up/op3=Climb-down layout. Climb-up now goes to the ice cave's
// real surface exterior (^waterbirth_ladder_surface_dest, Corey-supplied) instead of the rock
// cavern's steps.
[oploc1,dagannoth_ladder_base]
p_arrivedelay;
@ladder_options(^waterbirth_ladder_surface_dest, ^waterbirth_entrance_ladder_dest);

[oploc2,dagannoth_ladder_base]
p_arrivedelay;
~climb_ladder(^waterbirth_ladder_surface_dest, true);

[oploc3,dagannoth_ladder_base]
p_arrivedelay;
~climb_ladder(^waterbirth_entrance_ladder_dest, false);

[oploc1,dagannoth_ladder_base_ext2]
p_arrivedelay;
@ladder_options(^waterbirth_ladder_surface_dest, ^waterbirth_entrance_ladder_dest);

[oploc2,dagannoth_ladder_base_ext2]
p_arrivedelay;
~climb_ladder(^waterbirth_ladder_surface_dest, true);

[oploc3,dagannoth_ladder_base_ext2]
p_arrivedelay;
~climb_ladder(^waterbirth_entrance_ladder_dest, false);

// dagannoth_rocky_steps (real op1=Climb, name="Steps", desc="Leads to the surface" - explicit in the
// cache) is the genuine surface exit from the pressure-door room.
[oploc1,dagannoth_rocky_steps]
mes("You climb the steps and back out into the cold Rellekka air.");
p_telejump(^waterbirth_surface_return);

// dagexp_exit_ladder (m30_68, plane 3) is isolated like dagexp_entrance_ladder - no matching second
// placement found anywhere in this region. Originally guessed as a "way out" back to the surface
// (same as dagannoth_rocky_steps); Corey corrected this in-game (2026-09-03, round 5) - it actually
// connects straight into the Horror from the Deep underground complex (m39_72), the same complex
// reached from the lighthouse's own basement ladder (see quest_horror.constant's
// ^horror_maze_exit_dest and quest_horror.rs2's ladder section) - tying the quest's two entrances
// together into one connected dungeon rather than a dead-end back to Rellekka.
[oploc1,dagexp_exit_ladder]
mes("You climb up through a narrow passage and emerge into a dark, unfamiliar chamber.");
p_telejump(^horror_maze_exit_dest);

[oploc1,dagexp_entrance_ladder]
mes("You climb up the ladder.");
p_telejump(^waterbirth_ladder_return_dest);


// --- Pressure-door puzzle (m38_158) ---
// Three independent door+pad installations, ~16 tiles apart (not one shared three-door room). Each
// needs a player standing on both of its pads at once - since the two pads are always two distinct
// tiles, checking both independently returns >=1 each only when two different players are involved.
[oploc1,dagannoth_pressure_door]
if (map_playercount(^waterbirth_door1_pad1, ^waterbirth_door1_pad1) >= 1 & map_playercount(^waterbirth_door1_pad2, ^waterbirth_door1_pad2) >= 1) {
    mes("With a grinding of stone, the door slowly swings open!");
    loc_change(dagannoth_pressure_open, 100);
} else {
    mes("The door won't budge - it feels like it needs weight on both pressure pads beside it at once.");
}

[oploc1,dagannoth_pressure_door2]
if (map_playercount(^waterbirth_door2_pad1, ^waterbirth_door2_pad1) >= 1 & map_playercount(^waterbirth_door2_pad2, ^waterbirth_door2_pad2) >= 1) {
    mes("With a grinding of stone, the door slowly swings open!");
    loc_change(dagannoth_pressure_open, 100);
} else {
    mes("The door won't budge - it feels like it needs weight on both pressure pads beside it at once.");
}

[oploc1,dagannoth_pressure_door3]
if (map_playercount(^waterbirth_door3_pad1, ^waterbirth_door3_pad1) >= 1 & map_playercount(^waterbirth_door3_pad2, ^waterbirth_door3_pad2) >= 1) {
    mes("With a grinding of stone, the door slowly swings open!");
    loc_change(dagannoth_pressure_open, 100);
} else {
    mes("The door won't budge - it feels like it needs weight on both pressure pads beside it at once.");
}


// --- The Kings' lair connector (m29_68 <-> m45_69) ---
// dagexp_bossroomladder_down/_up: the one genuine cross-region pair in this whole network (same
// "bossroomladder" name, no local partner in either one's own region) - real, evidenced connector
// straight into the Kings' lair. dagannoth_kings.rs2's combat AI is already wired to the three npcs
// waiting on the other side.
[oploc1,dagexp_bossroomladder_down]
mes("You climb down into a vast, echoing chamber.");
p_telejump(^waterbirth_bossroom_down_dest);

[oploc1,dagexp_bossroomladder_up]
mes("You climb up out of the chamber.");
p_telejump(^waterbirth_bossroom_up_dest);


// --- The main cavern's ladder maze (m28_68/m29_68/m30_68, planes 0-3) ---
// 17 real climb pairs (dagexp_ladder1-34), each connecting two adjacent planes at the same tile (+-1)
// - see area_waterbirth.constant for the full derivation. This is a genuine maze, not a single
// corridor: every pair is scripted faithfully rather than collapsed into a shortcut, matching the
// wiki's description of navigating monster-filled multi-floor corridors between the entrance and the
// Kings. "Up"/"down" messages are derived from which side of each pair sits on the higher plane.
[oploc1,dagexp_ladder1]
mes("You climb down the ladder.");
p_telejump(^wb_ladder1_dest);

